title: GUI configuration
tags: Configuration [[Customising the GUI]]
kind: reference
summary: gui/config.toml — ports, programs to open files with, list page sizes, timings, ref seeds, and each panel's initial values.

`~/.config/metafolder/gui/config.toml`. The shipped file comments every key and
its default; this note says what each part is for.

A key or a table the GUI does not know stops it at startup, with a message naming
it: a typo, or a setting that no longer exists, would otherwise be read as "the
default". The one exception is `[panel-defaults.<type>]`, whose keys belong to the
panels.

|!Key or table |!For |
|`daemon-port` |the daemon to connect to, on `127.0.0.1` (default 7523, the daemon's own); `--daemon-port` overrides it |
|`gui-port` |the port the GUI's own server binds (default 7524); `--gui-port` overrides it, and `mf gui` reads this same value — there is no port file to discover |
|`open-with` |the programs `file:open-with` offers to open a file with (completion candidates, not a whitelist; default: the desktop's handler) |
|`[page-size]` |how many rows or tiles each list panel loads per window, by panel type: `metarecord-list` 100, `file` 150, `file-manager` and `treeref` 200 |
|`[settings]` |timings of the GUI itself: the daemon health poll, a reconcile's completion poll, the repository-list cache, the GUI's slow-log threshold ([[Slow log configuration]]), and where a video's thumbnail is taken (`video-thumbnail-percent`, [[GUI file endpoints]]) |
|`[panels]` |timings shared by every panel: the finder's and the live preview's debounce, the repos panel's task poll; `status-message-ms` and `status-error-ms` are left without effect since status messages stopped clearing themselves ([[Status bar and task bar]]) |
|`[ref-seeds]` |how the targets of a `ref` field are named where a value is typed or shown: which metarecords (a query) and by which columns, per field, `*` being the default rule ([[Ref value seeds]]) |
|`[completion]` |the separator joining the columns of a multi-column completion label |
|`[panel-defaults.<panel>]` |each panel type's initial values — the columns `metarecord-list` opens on, the fields its finder searches, the field `treeref` opens on, the text extensions and the zoom of `file`… — handed to that panel as `metafolder.defaults`. A custom panel type gets its own table the same way; the GUI knows nothing of the keys. Where a panel keeps a setting per workspace, the stored value wins |

Every key is optional: a key, or a whole table, left out keeps its default. The
file itself must exist ([[No runtime fallback]]).

The file is read once, at startup: a change needs a restart
([[Reloading the configuration]]).

!! A panel's initial values

What a panel opens on is configuration, not a constant of its code. Each
`[panel-defaults.<panel>]` table belongs to the panel type of that name:

```
[panel-defaults.metarecord-list]
columns       = "mfr_path:path mfr_type &version"
finder-fields = ["mfr_path:path", "label:value", "name:value"]

[panel-defaults.treeref]
field = "mfr_path"
```

* A key left out keeps the value built into the panel.
* A value the panel cannot use — a columns text that does not parse, a size that is not a number — is ignored in favour of the built-in one; it never breaks the panel.
* These are ''initial'' values. Where a panel keeps the setting per workspace — the columns and the finder fields of the list, the field of `treeref` — the configured value is only what a workspace starts with ([[Workspace variables]]).

What every panel shares stays in its own table: `[panels]` for the timings,
`[page-size]` for the page sizes.
